# HTTP controllers

Use a controller to group related Axum routes under one base path. Define the values that cross the wire, declare the controller, then add route methods in an inherent handler implementation.

## Define a controller and its first route

<Example title="Fetch a user by ID">

```rust
use axum::extract::Path;
use upwell::axum::prelude::*;

#[dto]
struct User {
    id: u64,
    name: String,
}
```

Declare the singleton controller and the base path shared by its routes.

```rust
#[controller(path = "/users")]
struct UsersController;
```

Add the route. Axum extractors such as `Path<T>` provide request data, while `Json<T>` writes a typed response.

```rust
use upwell::axum::prelude::{get, handlers, Path};

#[handlers]
impl UsersController {
    #[get("/{id}")]
    async fn get(&self, Path(id): Path<u64>) -> Json<User> {
        Json(User {
            id,
            name: String::from("Ada"),
        })
    }
}
```

</Example>

Register or auto-discover the controller in the Axum application configuration. `#[dto]` marks types used in request and response wire positions; it supplies serialization support unless the type already provides it.

## Add input and dependencies to a route

Use `Query<T>` for URL parameters, `Json<T>` for a JSON body, and `Inject<T>` for an application dependency. <Symbol path="upwell::axum::HttpRequest" /> is the request scope marker: components declared in that scope are created for one HTTP request and can depend on request-scoped state without leaking it across requests.

<Example title="Keep request state in the HTTP request scope">

```rust
use upwell::axum::prelude::*;

#[component(scope = HttpRequest)]
struct RequestAudit;
```

The request scope begins when the router dispatches a request and ends after its handler returns. A request-scoped component can depend on singleton services, but singleton components must not retain request-scoped values.

</Example>

<Example title="Search with query, body, and injected index">

```rust context
#[controller(path = "/users")]
struct UsersController;
```

```rust
use upwell::axum::prelude::*;

#[dto]
struct Page {
    limit: usize,
}

#[dto]
struct SearchRequest {
    query: String,
}

#[dto]
struct SearchResponse {
    results: Vec<String>,
}

#[component]
struct SearchIndex;

impl SearchIndex {
    async fn search(&self, page: Page, request: SearchRequest) -> SearchResponse {
        SearchResponse {
            results: vec![format!("{}:{}", request.query, page.limit)],
        }
    }
}
```

Extend the existing controller handler implementation with the new route.

```rust
#[handlers]
impl UsersController {
    #[post("/search")]
    async fn search(
        &self,
        Query(page): Query<Page>,
        Json(request): Json<SearchRequest>,
        Inject(index): Inject<SearchIndex>,
    ) -> Json<SearchResponse> {
        Json(index.search(page, request).await)
    }
}
```

</Example>

Use a direct typed response whenever possible. When a custom `IntoResponse` obscures the success body or alternatives, document that contract with route response metadata in the [`#[route]` reference](/docs/latest/symbols/upwell_axum_macros/route).

## Accept a request stream only for streamed input

Add one `#[stream]` parameter when the client sends a sequence rather than one JSON body.

<Example title="Ingest a stream of events">

```rust context
#[controller(path = "/users")]
struct UsersController;
```

```rust
use futures_core::Stream;
use upwell::axum::prelude::*;

#[dto]
struct Event {
    value: String,
}
```

Add the stream route to the controller.

```rust
#[handlers]
impl UsersController {
    #[post("/events")]
    async fn ingest(&self, #[stream] events: impl Stream<Item = Event>) -> StatusCode {
        let _ = events;
        StatusCode::ACCEPTED
    }
}
```

</Example>

A route has at most one request stream. Use a recognized streaming response, or `streamed` when a custom return type hides that shape, only when the client must consume results incrementally.

